dispatch blind peers over the disjoint slices; hunt candidate claims two ways; fan them all in.

.why = a code review asks "is this code good?"; a redteam asks "what reaches in, what could get
out, what's already out?". the grain does the work a review is blind to. blind, disjoint peers
catch compositional chains a shared theory would miss.

read `.claims/1.ground.yield.md` for the partition. dispatch one blind peer per slice (via the Agent
mechanism — fresh context per peer, no shared summary). each peer hunts ITS slice only.

each peer's prompt carries one history rule, for the fresh hunt and the re-detect alike: a hunt
that reads history excludes the repair's backup refs (`git log
--exclude='refs/redteam-backup/*' --all -S '<value>'`). a backup holds a redacted value by
design (the undo); a peer that reads it opens a new claim at a closed key, and the claim never
CLOSEs.

---

## 2.0 re-detect the prior cycle's repairs FIRST — the trust check the loop rests on

**this stone is the one that confirms a repair held.** the repair stone marks each fix
PENDING-REDETECT and earns it CLOSED only here; the promote stone's convergence test reads
"repairs held (re-detect confirmed)". so before the fresh hunt, re-detect every repair the last
cycle made — otherwise the whole repair-safety story references a check no stone performs.

read `.claims/0.cycle.md` (the cross-cycle record — named by convention so a fresh agent context
after a rewind finds it). it carries, per prior cycle, the `{channel}::{mechanism}` key of every
open claim — each PENDING-REDETECT repair and each critical claim still open. for each carried key:

- **re-hunt that exact channel + mechanism against the repaired tree** (re-run the command that
  banked the claim in the first place — the grep, the config read, the port probe). a leak key's
  re-hunt also reads history (`git log --exclude='refs/redteam-backup/*' --all -S '<value>'`): a value gone from HEAD but present in
  a commit still matches. every re-hunt excludes `$route/**` and every route's claims
  (`.behavior/*/.claims/**`): a claim record holds the value verbatim, so a re-hunt that reads
  one — this route's, or a prior or parallel sweep's — re-matches it and reports a repair that
  held as one that did not.
- a key whose channel + mechanism **no longer matches** → mark it **REDETECT-ABSENT**. the verify
  stone flips a REDETECT-ABSENT key to CLOSED (the repair held).
- a key that **still matches** → the claim stands. keep it an open claim, carried forward into
  this cycle's candidate set alongside the fresh hunt below — whether or not a fresh peer re-finds it.

a rewind resumes here with the cycle-1 partition, so the slices do not grow. each surface a
repair added (`## surface added` in the prior `.claims/4.repair.yield.md`, when it still stands)
joins the slice of the peer whose `confined-to` covers it; a surface no slice covers is hunted
apart and named in the yield, never left to nobody.

⚠️ **trust follows the re-detect, never a yield file.** read the keys from `.claims/0.cycle.md`, not
from `.claims/4.repair.yield.md` — the rewind that restarts this stone may archive the repair yield
out from under you, but `0.cycle.md` is written to survive it (see `5.promote.stone`). if there is no
prior cycle (the first pass), `0.cycle.md` carries no keys — proceed straight to the fresh hunt.

---

## each peer itemizes its findings TWO ways

### 2.1 detect.via_imagination
the peer reasons from its slice + first principles. the instinct that hunts PAST any checklist and
catches novel chains. itemize every candidate.

### 2.2 detect.via_research
the peer researches the current vuln landscape for the subjects on its slice, grounded on the
`inventory.of=vuln.class` corpus (known classes). cite each source **verbatim via the bhrowser**
(never WebFetch/WebSearch — see `rule.require.bhrowser-citations`). itemize every candidate.

on a later cycle, also read the prior cycle's `## accrued classes` in `.claims/5.promote.yield.md`
(the flywheel): each class listed there is a known class for this hunt, even before the upstream
corpus lands. absent on the first cycle; proceed without it.

if the `inventory.of=vuln.class` corpus is absent from this role, ground directly on the public
canon (OWASP Top 10, CWE Top 25, MITRE ATT&CK) via the bhrowser instead, and say so in the yield.

if the bhrowser itself is absent from this habitat (no bhrowser skill in `rhx list`), say so in the
yield and hold each via_research candidate as an UNCITED lead — verify confirms it against the
tree like any lead, and no claim cites a class it never read. never fall back to WebFetch/WebSearch.

> a checklist alone misses novel chains; instinct alone misses known classes. the value is the AND.

---

## 2.3 detect.blend — the TOTAL fan-in

merge the itemized candidates on **both** axes at once:
- **within each peer** — its 2.1 + 2.2 findings
- **across peers** — merge the claims two peers keyed alike, and link a chain they reached from
  opposite ends

**the dedup key = the (channel, mechanism) pair, NOT the prose.** two blind peers describe the same
chain in different words. same channel + same mechanism → one claim, merge. same channel +
different mechanism → two claims, keep both. this key doubles as the re-detect key later.

two ends of one chain (an entry seen by one peer, its exit by another) carry different keys by
construction, so the key never merges them. keep both claims, and give each a
`chain-with: {the other's key}` line; verify confirms the linked claims together, and a repair of
either end re-detects both keys.

blindness held through 2.1 / 2.2; blend is the ONE non-blind join.

---

## ⚠️ the KNOWN BLIND SPOT — the aggregate map (deferred)

blind, disjoint peers catch compositional chains, but they **cannot** see a leak that is a property
of the WHOLE corpus — the *map* (topology + principal names + retrieval commands + guardrail status)
that no single slice holds, yet the published SET discloses. a union reader whose scope is the whole
tree would see it, but that reader breaks the blindness the rest of the sweep depends on.

this v1 does **not** run that non-blind union pass — it is deferred (a design call the vision left
open). so blend must **flag** a suspected aggregate map as an open item for a separate non-blind
review, and must NOT report "no leaks, clean" on the strength of the blind slices alone. the map is
the highest-value leak and the one this method is weakest on; name the gap, never paper over it.

---

## every claim is a CHAIN, never a smell

| grain | the chain |
|-------|-----------|
| **infil** | entry point → mechanism → blast radius |
| **exfil** | exit channel → mechanism → blast radius \| audience |
| **leak**  | exposure → audience → payload |

record each claim into `$route/.claims/{grain}/{peer-slug}.md` (gitignored — a live claim NEVER
publishes). each cycle writes these files and the yield fresh: overwrite, never append, and remove
a prior cycle's per-peer file this cycle did not rewrite. a stale file from cycle N must never
stand in for cycle N+1's hunt. the per-grain namespace keeps the three disjoint grain-partitions from a filename clash
in one flat dir, and attributes each claim file to its grain + slice without a read. EVERY artifact
that names a claim — the per-claim records AND the yield below — lives under `.claims/`; no artifact
with a channel/mechanism/blast-radius lands in the tracked route root.

## emit

```
### claim: {short name}
- grain: {infil | exfil | leak}
- channel: {the entry/exit point, or the exposed-value location}
- mechanism: {what makes it exploitable}
- blast-radius | audience | payload: {the harm}
- found-by: {which peer(s), which method(s)}
- key: {channel}::{mechanism}   ← the dedup + re-detect key
```

and one line per peer, under `### out-of-scope reads`:

```
- {peer}: {none | each path it read outside its `confined-to`}
```

emit the candidate set into $route/.claims/2.detect.yield.md (all claim records live under .claims/)
